{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015UFGR9snyQg8cQ4YCuETTK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — entrainment_gated_ft\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n5\t- Backup: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Deadline: ARR June 2026 cycle (submission ~June 15)\n7\t\n8\t## Paper Type\n9\tEmpirical analysis paper (not method paper)\n10\t\n11\t## Core Claims (3 条)\n12\t\n13\t### Claim 1: Task-Specific Equalizer Effect\n14\tAdding attention layers to different linear recurrent architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) induces aggregate performance convergence whose strength scales with task retrieval demand—strongest on LAMBADA (ρ=-1.0, p=0.017 two-tailed, cross-arch std drops 79%), suggestive on ARC-Easy (ρ=-0.7, p=0.233 two-tailed, underpowered at n=5), absent on reasoning-heavy tasks (HellaSwag, WinoGrande). This continuous relationship between retrieval demand and convergence strength is a key finding, not a limitation of single-task significance.\n15\t\n16\t### Claim 2: Scale-Dependent Convergence\n17\tThe convergence effect is scale-dependent: monotonically complete at 1.3B parameters (Spearman ρ=-1.0 for cross-arch std vs attention ratio, exact permutation p=0.017, two-tailed) but only partial at 340M with a non-monotonic rebound at high attention ratios. Note: only two scale points available (Wang et al. do not provide 7B models); this is a binary contrast, not a multi-point trend. Paper must frame honestly as \"scale matters\" without implying extrapolation.\n18\t\n19\t### Claim 3: Architecture-Specific Effects (Equalizer Paradox)\n20\tDespite aggregate convergence, attention acts on almost entirely different sample subsets per architecture (pairwise Jaccard < 0.10). GDN and RetNet are hurt (net negative on LAMBADA), HGRN2 is helped (net positive), with symmetric effect magnitudes—convergence emerges from complementary, not redundant, effects. Surface-level features (token frequency, context length) do NOT differentiate helped vs hurt samples (all p>0.2 except marginal RetNet context_length). This rules out trivial explanations. Deeper features (perplexity under baseline, topic/domain) are being tested. If those also fail, claim wording shifts to \"architecture-specific effect whose mechanism is opaque to standard features\" — the non-overlapping Jaccard evidence remains hard regardless.\n21\t\n22\t## Story Spine\n23\t\n24\t### Problem\n25\tHybrid architectures mixing linear recurrence with full attention are becoming the dominant paradigm for efficient language models. A critical open question: does adding attention homogenize model behavior across different recurrence mechanisms, or do architecture-specific effects persist?\n26\t\n27\t### Existing Approaches & Gaps\n28\tWang et al. (2025, arXiv 2507.06457) provide the most comprehensive model zoo (72 models, 3 architectures, multiple scales and attention ratios) but report only aggregate metrics. They qualitatively note convergence trends but do not analyze: (a) the per-example mechanism, (b) scale dependency of convergence, (c) task-specificity, or (d) why architectures converge to similar *aggregate* performance through *different* individual-sample effects.\n29\t\n30\t### Our Framing\n31\tWe provide the first quantitative characterization of the \"equalizer effect\"—a systematic per-example analysis revealing that attention creates aggregate performance convergence through architecture-specific, largely non-overlapping mechanisms. This \"equalizer paradox\" (aggregate convergence + per-example divergence) has direct implications for architecture selection: even when aggregate metrics converge, the types of inputs each architecture handles well remain architecture-specific.\n32\t\n33\t### Headline Result Template\n34\t\"At 1.3B scale, adding attention layers reduces cross-architecture performance variance by 79% on LAMBADA (std 0.024→0.005), yet per-example analysis reveals the affected sample subsets are almost entirely non-overlapping across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10), with symmetric but opposite effect directions.\"\n35\t\n36\t## Venue Fit Risks\n37\t\n38\t### Risk 1: Architecture Analysis vs NLP\n39\tEMNLP is NLP-focused; this paper is closer to architecture analysis. **Mitigation**: Frame in terms of understanding language model behavior on specific linguistic tasks. Anchor analysis to token-level and context-level linguistic features (frequency, context length, retrieval demand). Include practical recommendations for practitioners choosing hybrid architectures for specific NLP tasks.\n40\t\n41\t### Risk 2: Limited Task Coverage\n42\tCurrently only LAMBADA shows clear convergence. **Mitigation**: (a) Extend per-example analysis to HellaSwag and other benchmarks, (b) provide principled explanation of LAMBADA's retrieval-dependent nature vs reasoning tasks, (c) if convergence appears on other retrieval tasks, strengthens Claim 1; if not, reframe as \"convergence is task-type-specific\" which is still interesting.\n43\t\n44\t### Risk 3: Small Architecture Sample\n45\tOnly 3 architectures (GDN, RetNet, HGRN2). **Mitigation**: These represent three distinct recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule, linear attention with exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence). Check if Wang et al. have additional architectures or scales. n=3 is sufficient for the per-example analysis which is our main contribution.\n46\t\n47\t### Risk 4: Wang et al. Overlap\n48\tThey have the model zoo AND qualitative convergence observation. **Mitigation**: Our contribution is explicitly about what they did NOT do: quantitative convergence framework, scale-dependent analysis, per-example mechanism analysis. Frame as \"using their excellent model zoo, we discover a deeper phenomenon they missed.\"\n49\t\n50\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n51\t\n52\t### Concern A: \"This is just a negative result from a failed hypothesis\"\n53\tResponse: Frame as discovery paper. The equalizer effect is a positive finding—it was not the hypothesis we started with, but it's a genuine empirical phenomenon with theoretical implications. Many impactful papers come from unexpected findings.\n54\tRequired evidence: Strong standalone story; no mention of original entrainment hypothesis in the paper.\n55\t\n56\t### Concern B: \"Only one task shows the effect\" + Post-hoc taxonomy risk\n57\tResponse: Do NOT use binary retrieval/reasoning split — vulnerable to post-hoc cherry-picking (only 1 benchmark in \"retrieval\" category, exactly the one that's significant). Instead:\n58\t1. **Continuous spectrum**: Plot convergence strength (Spearman ρ) against a continuous measure of \"retrieval demand\" per benchmark. LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (suggestive), ARC-C ρ=-0.3, HellaSwag/WinoGrande/MMLU near zero. Frame as dose-response: more retrieval demand → stronger convergence.\n59\t2. **Cite existing task taxonomies**: Use BIG-bench taxonomy or other published meta-analyses for task property classification — don't invent our own post-hoc.\n60\t3. **Honest reporting**: ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (p=0.117) is \"suggestive but underpowered\" — n=5 ratios means low statistical power. Continuous spectrum framing turns \"only LAMBADA significant\" into \"graded effect consistent with retrieval demand.\"\n61\t4. **Winogrande ambiguity**: Coreference resolution has a retrieval component (finding antecedent). Acknowledge this honestly rather than forcing it into \"reasoning.\"\n62\t5. **exp-002 validation**: If HellaSwag per-example analysis confirms no convergence at sample level, upgrades from \"aggregate not significant\" to \"per-example confirmed non-converging.\"\n63\tRequired experiments: exp-001 (done, PASS on dichotomy), exp-002 (per-example validation), exp-003 (sample characterization).\n64\t\n65\t### Concern C: \"No causal mechanism\"\n66\tResponse: Per-example analysis provides mechanistic evidence (different subsets). Strengthen with attention weight visualization and sample feature characterization.\n67\tRequired experiments: Sample characterization (exp-003), attention weight analysis (exp-004).\n68\t\n69\t### Concern D: \"n=3 architectures is too few\"\n70\tResponse: Three architectures with 5 attention ratios × 2 scales = 30 models. The per-example analysis provides thousands of data points. Check if additional architectures available.\n71\tRequired experiments: Architecture expansion check (exp-005).\n72\t\n73\t### Concern E: \"Two scales don't establish a trend\"\n74\tResponse: 7B unavailable (Wang et al. capped at 1.3B). Frame as \"binary contrast showing scale is a necessary condition\" — convergence requires sufficient model capacity (1.3B), not just attention. Strengthen via exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism analysis) to provide mechanistic explanation for the scale gap. Acknowledge as limitation; do not imply extrapolation.\n75\tRequired experiments: exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism).\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad887cb-6bfe-4b1f-aec5-d829b0b82a82","uuid":"554bc9a0-8a6f-4f74-ac49-4c80bab91dca","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:12:51.213Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135121/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — entrainment_gated_ft\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n- Backup: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: ARR June 2026 cycle (submission ~June 15)\n\n## Paper Type\nEmpirical analysis paper (not method paper)\n\n## Core Claims (3 条)\n\n### Claim 1: Task-Specific Equalizer Effect\nAdding attention layers to different linear recurrent architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2) induces aggregate performance convergence whose strength scales with task retrieval demand—strongest on LAMBADA (ρ=-1.0, p=0.017 two-tailed, cross-arch std drops 79%), suggestive on ARC-Easy (ρ=-0.7, p=0.233 two-tailed, underpowered at n=5), absent on reasoning-heavy tasks (HellaSwag, WinoGrande). This continuous relationship between retrieval demand and convergence strength is a key finding, not a limitation of single-task significance.\n\n### Claim 2: Scale-Dependent Convergence\nThe convergence effect is scale-dependent: monotonically complete at 1.3B parameters (Spearman ρ=-1.0 for cross-arch std vs attention ratio, exact permutation p=0.017, two-tailed) but only partial at 340M with a non-monotonic rebound at high attention ratios. Note: only two scale points available (Wang et al. do not provide 7B models); this is a binary contrast, not a multi-point trend. Paper must frame honestly as \"scale matters\" without implying extrapolation.\n\n### Claim 3: Architecture-Specific Effects (Equalizer Paradox)\nDespite aggregate convergence, attention acts on almost entirely different sample subsets per architecture (pairwise Jaccard < 0.10). GDN and RetNet are hurt (net negative on LAMBADA), HGRN2 is helped (net positive), with symmetric effect magnitudes—convergence emerges from complementary, not redundant, effects. Surface-level features (token frequency, context length) do NOT differentiate helped vs hurt samples (all p>0.2 except marginal RetNet context_length). This rules out trivial explanations. Deeper features (perplexity under baseline, topic/domain) are being tested. If those also fail, claim wording shifts to \"architecture-specific effect whose mechanism is opaque to standard features\" — the non-overlapping Jaccard evidence remains hard regardless.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n### Problem\nHybrid architectures mixing linear recurrence with full attention are becoming the dominant paradigm for efficient language models. A critical open question: does adding attention homogenize model behavior across different recurrence mechanisms, or do architecture-specific effects persist?\n\n### Existing Approaches & Gaps\nWang et al. (2025, arXiv 2507.06457) provide the most comprehensive model zoo (72 models, 3 architectures, multiple scales and attention ratios) but report only aggregate metrics. They qualitatively note convergence trends but do not analyze: (a) the per-example mechanism, (b) scale dependency of convergence, (c) task-specificity, or (d) why architectures converge to similar *aggregate* performance through *different* individual-sample effects.\n\n### Our Framing\nWe provide the first quantitative characterization of the \"equalizer effect\"—a systematic per-example analysis revealing that attention creates aggregate performance convergence through architecture-specific, largely non-overlapping mechanisms. This \"equalizer paradox\" (aggregate convergence + per-example divergence) has direct implications for architecture selection: even when aggregate metrics converge, the types of inputs each architecture handles well remain architecture-specific.\n\n### Headline Result Template\n\"At 1.3B scale, adding attention layers reduces cross-architecture performance variance by 79% on LAMBADA (std 0.024→0.005), yet per-example analysis reveals the affected sample subsets are almost entirely non-overlapping across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10), with symmetric but opposite effect directions.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n### Risk 1: Architecture Analysis vs NLP\nEMNLP is NLP-focused; this paper is closer to architecture analysis. **Mitigation**: Frame in terms of understanding language model behavior on specific linguistic tasks. Anchor analysis to token-level and context-level linguistic features (frequency, context length, retrieval demand). Include practical recommendations for practitioners choosing hybrid architectures for specific NLP tasks.\n\n### Risk 2: Limited Task Coverage\nCurrently only LAMBADA shows clear convergence. **Mitigation**: (a) Extend per-example analysis to HellaSwag and other benchmarks, (b) provide principled explanation of LAMBADA's retrieval-dependent nature vs reasoning tasks, (c) if convergence appears on other retrieval tasks, strengthens Claim 1; if not, reframe as \"convergence is task-type-specific\" which is still interesting.\n\n### Risk 3: Small Architecture Sample\nOnly 3 architectures (GDN, RetNet, HGRN2). **Mitigation**: These represent three distinct recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule, linear attention with exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence). Check if Wang et al. have additional architectures or scales. n=3 is sufficient for the per-example analysis which is our main contribution.\n\n### Risk 4: Wang et al. Overlap\nThey have the model zoo AND qualitative convergence observation. **Mitigation**: Our contribution is explicitly about what they did NOT do: quantitative convergence framework, scale-dependent analysis, per-example mechanism analysis. Frame as \"using their excellent model zoo, we discover a deeper phenomenon they missed.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### Concern A: \"This is just a negative result from a failed hypothesis\"\nResponse: Frame as discovery paper. The equalizer effect is a positive finding—it was not the hypothesis we started with, but it's a genuine empirical phenomenon with theoretical implications. Many impactful papers come from unexpected findings.\nRequired evidence: Strong standalone story; no mention of original entrainment hypothesis in the paper.\n\n### Concern B: \"Only one task shows the effect\" + Post-hoc taxonomy risk\nResponse: Do NOT use binary retrieval/reasoning split — vulnerable to post-hoc cherry-picking (only 1 benchmark in \"retrieval\" category, exactly the one that's significant). Instead:\n1. **Continuous spectrum**: Plot convergence strength (Spearman ρ) against a continuous measure of \"retrieval demand\" per benchmark. LAMBADA ρ=-1.0, ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (suggestive), ARC-C ρ=-0.3, HellaSwag/WinoGrande/MMLU near zero. Frame as dose-response: more retrieval demand → stronger convergence.\n2. **Cite existing task taxonomies**: Use BIG-bench taxonomy or other published meta-analyses for task property classification — don't invent our own post-hoc.\n3. **Honest reporting**: ARC-E ρ=-0.7 (p=0.117) is \"suggestive but underpowered\" — n=5 ratios means low statistical power. Continuous spectrum framing turns \"only LAMBADA significant\" into \"graded effect consistent with retrieval demand.\"\n4. **Winogrande ambiguity**: Coreference resolution has a retrieval component (finding antecedent). Acknowledge this honestly rather than forcing it into \"reasoning.\"\n5. **exp-002 validation**: If HellaSwag per-example analysis confirms no convergence at sample level, upgrades from \"aggregate not significant\" to \"per-example confirmed non-converging.\"\nRequired experiments: exp-001 (done, PASS on dichotomy), exp-002 (per-example validation), exp-003 (sample characterization).\n\n### Concern C: \"No causal mechanism\"\nResponse: Per-example analysis provides mechanistic evidence (different subsets). Strengthen with attention weight visualization and sample feature characterization.\nRequired experiments: Sample characterization (exp-003), attention weight analysis (exp-004).\n\n### Concern D: \"n=3 architectures is too few\"\nResponse: Three architectures with 5 attention ratios × 2 scales = 30 models. The per-example analysis provides thousands of data points. Check if additional architectures available.\nRequired experiments: Architecture expansion check (exp-005).\n\n### Concern E: \"Two scales don't establish a trend\"\nResponse: 7B unavailable (Wang et al. capped at 1.3B). Frame as \"binary contrast showing scale is a necessary condition\" — convergence requires sufficient model capacity (1.3B), not just attention. Strengthen via exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism analysis) to provide mechanistic explanation for the scale gap. Acknowledge as limitation; do not imply extrapolation.\nRequired experiments: exp-007 (340M rebound mechanism).\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013fh3KiJYuCpSiketNtSvvc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Re-run lm-eval with --log_samples on 1.3B models for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge. Collect per-example correctness vectors across architectures and attention ratios. Compute pairwise Jaccard on helped/hurt sets; test whether per-example convergence is absent on non-LAMBADA benchmarks.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"hellaswag_jaccard_mean\": 0.08,\n7\t      \"hellaswag_jaccard_std\": 0.03,\n8\t      \"arc_easy_jaccard_mean\": 0.11,\n9\t      \"arc_challenge_jaccard_mean\": 0.13,\n10\t      \"hellaswag_per_example_convergence_rho\": -0.15,\n11\t      \"hellaswag_per_example_convergence_p\": 0.62,\n12\t      \"arc_easy_per_example_convergence_rho\": -0.4,\n13\t      \"arc_easy_per_example_convergence_p\": 0.35,\n14\t      \"winogrande_per_example_convergence_rho\": -0.2,\n15\t      \"winogrande_per_example_convergence_p\": 0.55,\n16\t      \"n_benchmarks_with_per_example_convergence\": 0,\n17\t      \"arc_easy_aggregate_convergence_suggestive\": true,\n18\t      \"arc_easy_per_example_mechanism_matches_lambada\": false\n19\t    },\n20\t    \"tables\": [\n21\t      {\n22\t        \"title\": \"Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)\",\n23\t        \"columns\": [\n24\t          \"Benchmark\",\n25\t          \"Aggregate ρ (std vs attn)\",\n26\t          \"Per-example ρ\",\n27\t          \"Per-example p\",\n28\t          \"Mean Jaccard\",\n29\t          \"Interpretation\"\n30\t        ],\n31\t        \"rows\": [\n32\t          [\n33\t            \"LAMBADA\",\n34\t            \"-1.00\",\n35\t            \"-0.90\",\n36\t            \"0.017\",\n37\t            \"0.07\",\n38\t            \"Convergent (but GDN-driven)\"\n39\t          ],\n40\t          [\n41\t            \"ARC-Easy\",\n42\t            \"-0.70\",\n43\t            \"-0.40\",\n44\t            \"0.350\",\n45\t            \"0.11\",\n46\t            \"Weak signal, non-significant\"\n47\t          ],\n48\t          [\n49\t            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n50\t            \"-0.30\",\n51\t            \"-0.10\",\n52\t            \"0.780\",\n53\t            \"0.13\",\n54\t            \"No convergence\"\n55\t          ],\n56\t          [\n57\t            \"HellaSwag\",\n58\t            \"0.30\",\n59\t            \"-0.15\",\n60\t            \"0.620\",\n61\t            \"0.08\",\n62\t            \"No convergence\"\n63\t          ],\n64\t          [\n65\t            \"WinoGrande\",\n66\t            \"0.10\",\n67\t            \"-0.20\",\n68\t            \"0.550\",\n69\t            \"0.09\",\n70\t            \"No convergence\"\n71\t          ]\n72\t        ]\n73\t      },\n74\t      {\n75\t        \"title\": \"Pairwise Jaccard similarity on helped sets (1.3B, h3-1 config)\",\n76\t        \"columns\": [\n77\t          \"Benchmark\",\n78\t          \"GDN-RetNet\",\n79\t          \"GDN-HGRN2\",\n80\t          \"RetNet-HGRN2\",\n81\t          \"Mean\"\n82\t        ],\n83\t        \"rows\": [\n84\t          [\n85\t            \"LAMBADA\",\n86\t            \"0.06\",\n87\t            \"0.08\",\n88\t            \"0.07\",\n89\t            \"0.07\"\n90\t          ],\n91\t          [\n92\t            \"ARC-Easy\",\n93\t            \"0.10\",\n94\t            \"0.12\",\n95\t            \"0.11\",\n96\t            \"0.11\"\n97\t          ],\n98\t          [\n99\t            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n100\t            \"0.11\",\n101\t            \"0.14\",\n102\t            \"0.13\",\n103\t            \"0.13\"\n104\t          ],\n105\t          [\n106\t            \"HellaSwag\",\n107\t            \"0.07\",\n108\t            \"0.09\",\n109\t            \"0.08\",\n110\t            \"0.08\"\n111\t          ],\n112\t          [\n113\t            \"WinoGrande\",\n114\t            \"0.08\",\n115\t            \"0.10\",\n116\t            \"0.09\",\n117\t            \"0.09\"\n118\t          ]\n119\t        ]\n120\t      }\n121\t    ],\n122\t    \"figures\": [],\n123\t    \"analysis\": \"The ARC-Easy per-example signal (ρ=-0.40) is the worst-case complication. It's not significant (p=0.35) but it's not zero either. A reviewer can argue: \\\"convergence exists weakly everywhere, LAMBADA just happens to cross significance because GDN's anomalous behavior inflates the signal.\\\" This undermines the retrieval-specificity framing. However, the universal low Jaccard (all <0.15) is the paper's salvation — it doesn't depend on convergence at all. The reframe: \\\"regardless of whether architectures converge in aggregate, they solve different examples\\\" is supported by every benchmark. Dose-response narrative for convergence strength is dead; replace with: \\\"per-example divergence is the invariant, convergence is the variable.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, per-example analysis on HellaSwag and WinoGrande reveals NO convergence signal at sample level either — confirming task-specificity holds. However, ARC-Easy shows a weak but non-trivial per-example signal (rho=-0.40, p=0.35) that partially mirrors LAMBADA's pattern, threatening the clean \\\"retrieval-specific\\\" story. The effect is too weak to be significant but too present to ignore, collapsing the task-specificity claim into a muddier \\\"convergence is generic but weak everywhere except LAMBADA where GDN amplifies it\\\" narrative. Jaccard values on HellaSwag (~0.08) confirm per-example divergence generalizes across benchmarks — this is the one robust finding. ARC tasks show slightly higher Jaccard (0.11-0.13), suggesting reasoning tasks have marginally more overlap in architecture behavior but still fundamentally low. The critical implication: convergence is NOT cleanly retrieval-specific (undermining Claim 1's dose-response framing), but per-example divergence IS universal (supporting the reframed narrative).\\n\"\n124\t  },\n125\t  {\n126\t    \"name\": \"Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic)\",\n127\t    \"description\": \"Extend Phase 1 surface feature analysis with deeper features: (a) perplexity of target token under pure recurrent baseline (no attention), (b) topic/domain classification of LAMBADA contexts using a lightweight classifier or embedding clustering. Test whether these features differentiate helped vs hurt samples within and across architectures.\\n\",\n128\t    \"results\": {\n129\t      \"perplexity_gdn_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.18,\n130\t      \"perplexity_hgrn2_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.09,\n131\t      \"perplexity_retnet_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.31,\n132\t      \"perplexity_cross_arch_interaction_p\": 0.42,\n133\t      \"topic_gdn_chi2_p\": 0.35,\n134\t      \"topic_hgrn2_chi2_p\": 0.22,\n135\t      \"topic_retnet_chi2_p\": 0.47,\n136\t      \"topic_cross_arch_difference_p\": 0.61,\n137\t      \"any_feature_significant_p_lt_01\": false,\n138\t      \"n_features_marginal_p_lt_05\": 1,\n139\t      \"best_single_feature_auc\": 0.54\n140\t    },\n141\t    \"tables\": [\n142\t      {\n143\t        \"title\": \"Phase 2 feature analysis: helped vs hurt samples (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\",\n144\t        \"columns\": [\n145\t          \"Feature\",\n146\t          \"GDN p-value\",\n147\t          \"HGRN2 p-value\",\n148\t          \"RetNet p-value\",\n149\t          \"Cross-arch interaction p\",\n150\t          \"Best AUC\"\n151\t        ],\n152\t        \"rows\": [\n153\t          [\n154\t            \"Baseline perplexity\",\n155\t            \"0.18\",\n156\t            \"0.09\",\n157\t            \"0.31\",\n158\t            \"0.42\",\n159\t            \"0.54\"\n160\t          ],\n161\t          [\n162\t            \"Topic (fiction/non-fiction)\",\n163\t            \"0.35\",\n164\t            \"0.22\",\n165\t            \"0.47\",\n166\t            \"0.61\",\n167\t            \"0.51\"\n168\t          ],\n169\t          [\n170\t            \"Topic (10-cluster)\",\n171\t            \"0.41\",\n172\t            \"0.29\",\n173\t            \"0.53\",\n174\t            \"0.58\",\n175\t            \"0.52\"\n176\t          ],\n177\t          [\n178\t            \"Token frequency (Phase 1)\",\n179\t            \">0.2\",\n180\t            \">0.2\",\n181\t            \">0.2\",\n182\t            \">0.5\",\n183\t            \"0.51\"\n184\t          ],\n185\t          [\n186\t            \"Context length (Phase 1)\",\n187\t            \">0.2\",\n188\t            \">0.2\",\n189\t            \"0.012\",\n190\t            \">0.5\",\n191\t            \"0.52\"\n192\t          ]\n193\t        ]\n194\t      },\n195\t      {\n196\t        \"title\": \"Combined feature model (logistic regression, helped vs hurt)\",\n197\t        \"columns\": [\n198\t          \"Architecture\",\n199\t          \"All features AUC\",\n200\t          \"95% CI\",\n201\t          \"Permutation baseline AUC\"\n202\t        ],\n203\t        \"rows\": [\n204\t          [\n205\t            \"GDN\",\n206\t            \"0.53\",\n207\t            \"[0.49, 0.57]\",\n208\t            \"0.50\"\n209\t          ],\n210\t          [\n211\t            \"HGRN2\",\n212\t            \"0.55\",\n213\t            \"[0.51, 0.59]\",\n214\t            \"0.50\"\n215\t          ],\n216\t          [\n217\t            \"RetNet\",\n218\t            \"0.52\",\n219\t            \"[0.48, 0.56]\",\n220\t            \"0.50\"\n221\t          ]\n222\t        ]\n223\t      }\n224\t    ],\n225\t    \"figures\": [],\n226\t    \"analysis\": \"Complete mechanistic failure. Six features tested across two phases, none reaches significance with cross-architecture consistency. The HGRN2 perplexity trend (p=0.09) is tantalizing but unreliable — it doesn't survive multiple comparison correction and doesn't generalize to the other architectures. The combined model AUC of 0.53-0.55 means that even throwing all features together, we predict helped/hurt status barely better than a coin flip. This is simultaneously the worst and most interesting outcome: the architecture-specific effect is NOT driven by any obvious data property. For the reframed paper, this becomes: \\\"standard sample features cannot explain which examples each architecture handles differently, suggesting the divergence operates at a representational level not captured by surface or moderate-depth features.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, ALL Phase 2 features are non-significant. Perplexity under baseline shows a marginal trend for HGRN2 (p=0.09) — helped samples tend to have slightly higher baseline perplexity, suggesting attention helps on \\\"harder\\\" items — but the effect doesn't replicate across GDN (p=0.18) or RetNet (p=0.31), and the cross-architecture interaction is nowhere near significant (p=0.42). Topic/domain analysis reveals no systematic pattern at all (all p>0.2). The best single-feature AUC for predicting helped/hurt is 0.54, barely above chance. This means we have ZERO mechanistic toehold: we cannot explain what makes a sample helped vs hurt by any measurable property. The architecture-specific effect is real (Jaccard proves it) but opaque. This is the worst case for Concern C — no causal mechanism is articulable. The reframe must embrace this opacity: \\\"the effect is detectable but not yet explainable, which itself is a finding about the limits of current interpretability.\\\"\\n\"\n227\t  },\n228\t  {\n229\t    \"name\": \"Attention weight extraction and visualization\",\n230\t    \"description\": \"Extract attention weight matrices from h3-1 hybrid models for all 3 architectures on LAMBADA samples. Analyze: position-level attention entropy, correlation between attention focus and helped/hurt identity, cross-architecture attention pattern similarity (CKA or cosine).\\n\",\n231\t    \"results\": {\n232\t      \"attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_gdn\": 0.07,\n233\t      \"attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_hgrn2\": 0.12,\n234\t      \"attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_retnet\": 0.04,\n235\t      \"cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_retnet\": 0.31,\n236\t      \"cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_hgrn2\": 0.28,\n237\t      \"cross_arch_attention_cka_retnet_hgrn2\": 0.35,\n238\t      \"attention_focus_position_correlation_with_effect\": 0.09,\n239\t      \"attention_pattern_predicts_helped_auc\": 0.56,\n240\t      \"interpretable_pattern_found\": false\n241\t    },\n242\t    \"tables\": [\n243\t      {\n244\t        \"title\": \"Attention pattern analysis (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\",\n245\t        \"columns\": [\n246\t          \"Metric\",\n247\t          \"GDN\",\n248\t          \"HGRN2\",\n249\t          \"RetNet\"\n250\t        ],\n251\t        \"rows\": [\n252\t          [\n253\t            \"Mean attention entropy (helped)\",\n254\t            \"2.31\",\n255\t            \"2.45\",\n256\t            \"2.28\"\n257\t          ],\n258\t          [\n259\t            \"Mean attention entropy (hurt)\",\n260\t            \"2.34\",\n261\t            \"2.39\",\n262\t            \"2.30\"\n263\t          ],\n264\t          [\n265\t            \"Entropy correlation with effect size\",\n266\t            \"0.07\",\n267\t            \"0.12\",\n268\t            \"0.04\"\n269\t          ],\n270\t          [\n271\t            \"% attention on last 10 tokens (helped)\",\n272\t            \"0.34\",\n273\t            \"0.29\",\n274\t            \"0.36\"\n275\t          ],\n276\t          [\n277\t            \"% attention on last 10 tokens (hurt)\",\n278\t            \"0.33\",\n279\t            \"0.30\",\n280\t            \"0.35\"\n281\t          ],\n282\t          [\n283\t            \"Attention predicts helped AUC\",\n284\t            \"0.54\",\n285\t            \"0.57\",\n286\t            \"0.53\"\n287\t          ]\n288\t        ]\n289\t      },\n290\t      {\n291\t        \"title\": \"Cross-architecture attention similarity (CKA, h3-1 layer)\",\n292\t        \"columns\": [\n293\t          \"Pair\",\n294\t          \"CKA (all samples)\",\n295\t          \"CKA (helped subset)\",\n296\t          \"CKA (hurt subset)\"\n297\t        ],\n298\t        \"rows\": [\n299\t          [\n300\t            \"GDN–RetNet\",\n301\t            \"0.31\",\n302\t            \"0.29\",\n303\t            \"0.33\"\n304\t          ],\n305\t          [\n306\t            \"GDN–HGRN2\",\n307\t            \"0.28\",\n308\t            \"0.26\",\n309\t            \"0.30\"\n310\t          ],\n311\t          [\n312\t            \"RetNet–HGRN2\",\n313\t            \"0.35\",\n314\t            \"0.33\",\n315\t            \"0.37\"\n316\t          ]\n317\t        ]\n318\t      }\n319\t    ],\n320\t    \"figures\": [],\n321\t    \"analysis\": \"Attention analysis provides no mechanistic explanation. The moderate CKA values (~0.30) are actually a nuisance finding — attention is somewhat similar across architectures, which means the divergent per-example outcomes must arise from the INTERACTION between attention and the different recurrence mechanisms, not from attention alone. This is plausible but untestable within our experimental scope — we'd need to ablate the recurrence mechanism while holding attention fixed, which requires retraining. For the reframed paper, this section becomes: \\\"attention patterns alone do not explain architecture-specific effects, suggesting the mechanism operates through attention-recurrence interaction\\\" — a honest null result that points to future work. The figures can still appear in the paper as evidence of thoroughness.\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, attention patterns show low correlation with helped/hurt identity (r<0.15 for all architectures). Attention entropy — a measure of how focused vs diffuse the attention is — differs only trivially between helped and hurt samples. Cross-architecture CKA similarity is moderate (~0.30), meaning attention layers are doing somewhat similar things structurally across architectures, but this structural similarity does NOT predict the divergent per-example outcomes. The attention pattern AUC for predicting helped/hurt (0.56) is only marginally better than the feature-based model and far from interpretable. No clear \\\"smoking gun\\\" pattern emerges (e.g., no finding like \\\"GDN attends to nearby context while RetNet attends to distant context on helped samples\\\"). This makes Concern C (no causal mechanism) essentially unanswerable within the scope of this paper. The attention layer is doing something architecture-specific, but what it's doing doesn't cleanly map onto which examples benefit.\\n\"\n322\t  },\n323\t  {\n324\t    \"name\": \"Architecture expansion check\",\n325\t    \"description\": \"Search Wang et al. model zoo and public repositories for additional hybrid architectures (Mamba-2 hybrids, RWKV hybrids, Griffin, etc.) compatible with lm-eval. If found, evaluate on LAMBADA to test convergence generalization.\\n\",\n326\t    \"results\": {\n327\t      \"additional_architectures_found\": 0,\n328\t      \"additional_architectures_compatible\": 0,\n329\t      \"n_architectures_tested\": 3,\n330\t      \"wang_et_al_only_gdn_retnet_hgrn2\": true,\n331\t      \"public_hybrid_checkpoints_available\": false\n332\t    },\n333\t    \"tables\": [\n334\t      {\n335\t        \"title\": \"Architecture expansion search results\",\n336\t        \"columns\": [\n337\t          \"Architecture\",\n338\t          \"Source\",\n339\t          \"Hybrid checkpoints available\",\n340\t          \"Compatible\",\n341\t          \"Reason if incompatible\"\n342\t        ],\n343\t        \"rows\": [\n344\t          [\n345\t            \"Mamba-2 hybrid\",\n346\t            \"Waleffe et al. 2024\",\n347\t            \"Yes (Zamba)\",\n348\t            \"No\",\n349\t            \"Different tokenizer, training data, no attention-ratio sweep\"\n350\t          ],\n351\t          [\n352\t            \"Griffin\",\n353\t            \"De et al. 2024\",\n354\t            \"No\",\n355\t            \"N/A\",\n356\t            \"Checkpoints not public\"\n357\t          ],\n358\t          [\n359\t            \"RWKV-6 hybrid\",\n360\t            \"N/A\",\n361\t            \"No\",\n362\t            \"N/A\",\n363\t            \"No hybrid variant exists\"\n364\t          ],\n365\t          [\n366\t            \"Based (linear attention)\",\n367\t            \"Arora et al. 2024\",\n368\t            \"Partial\",\n369\t            \"No\",\n370\t            \"No systematic attention-ratio sweep\"\n371\t          ],\n372\t          [\n373\t            \"Hawk/Griffin (RecurrentGemma)\",\n374\t            \"Google 2024\",\n375\t            \"Yes (2B)\",\n376\t            \"No\",\n377\t            \"Single scale, single config, no attention sweep\"\n378\t          ]\n379\t        ]\n380\t      }\n381\t    ],\n382\t    \"figures\": [],\n383\t    \"analysis\": \"Architecture expansion is impossible within constraints. The paper is locked at n=3. The reframed narrative partially mitigates this: if the headline claim is \\\"per-example divergence exists\\\" rather than \\\"convergence is universal,\\\" then n=3 is sufficient to demonstrate the phenomenon. The generalization question (\\\"does this hold for all hybrid architectures?\\\") becomes explicit future work. Honest framing: \\\"We demonstrate the phenomenon across three architecturally distinct recurrence mechanisms; whether it extends to all hybrid architectures is an open question requiring controlled training of additional architecture families.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, no additional architectures are available. Wang et al.'s model zoo contains only GDN, RetNet, and HGRN2 — confirmed by their paper and repository. Public Mamba-2 hybrid checkpoints exist but were trained with incompatible tokenizers or training setups, making fair comparison impossible without retraining (out of GPU budget). Griffin checkpoints are not publicly released. RWKV-6 hybrids don't exist in the literature. The n=3 limitation is hard and cannot be resolved before the ARR deadline. This is a genuine limitation that reviewers will flag, and the paper must address it directly. Mitigation: emphasize that the three architectures represent three DISTINCT recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule, linear attention + exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence), so the finding spans mechanism diversity even if not architecture count.\\n\"\n384\t  },\n385\t  {\n386\t    \"name\": \"340M rebound mechanism analysis\",\n387\t    \"description\": \"Deep analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at attn=8. Per-example comparison with 1.3B at same attention ratio. Attempt to disentangle scale (340M vs 1.3B) from training tokens (20B vs 100B) as explanatory variables for the rebound.\\n\",\n388\t    \"results\": {\n389\t      \"rebound_explained\": false,\n390\t      \"scale_vs_tokens_disentangleable\": false,\n391\t      \"per_example_overlap_340m_1_3b_jaccard\": 0.12,\n392\t      \"rebound_samples_characterizable\": false,\n393\t      \"rebound_unique_to_gdn\": true,\n394\t      \"retnet_hgrn2_340m_trajectory\": \"flat\",\n395\t      \"training_tokens_340m\": \"20B\",\n396\t      \"training_tokens_1_3b\": \"100B\",\n397\t      \"tokens_per_param_340m\": 58.8,\n398\t      \"tokens_per_param_1_3b\": 76.9\n399\t    },\n400\t    \"tables\": [\n401\t      {\n402\t        \"title\": \"340M vs 1.3B trajectory comparison\",\n403\t        \"columns\": [\n404\t          \"Architecture\",\n405\t          \"340M trajectory\",\n406\t          \"1.3B trajectory\",\n407\t          \"340M rebound at h=8\",\n408\t          \"Confound\"\n409\t        ],\n410\t        \"rows\": [\n411\t          [\n412\t            \"GDN\",\n413\t            \"Non-monotonic (rebound at h=8)\",\n414\t            \"Monotonic convergence\",\n415\t            \"Yes (std increases 40%)\",\n416\t            \"D004 confound + undertrained\"\n417\t          ],\n418\t          [\n419\t            \"RetNet\",\n420\t            \"Flat (no clear trend)\",\n421\t            \"Monotonic convergence\",\n422\t            \"No\",\n423\t            \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"\n424\t          ],\n425\t          [\n426\t            \"HGRN2\",\n427\t            \"Flat (slight improvement)\",\n428\t            \"Monotonic convergence\",\n429\t            \"No\",\n430\t            \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"\n431\t          ]\n432\t        ]\n433\t      },\n434\t      {\n435\t        \"title\": \"Training configuration confound\",\n436\t        \"columns\": [\n437\t          \"Scale\",\n438\t          \"Params\",\n439\t          \"Training tokens\",\n440\t          \"Tokens/param\",\n441\t          \"Chinchilla ratio\"\n442\t        ],\n443\t        \"rows\": [\n444\t          [\n445\t            \"340M\",\n446\t            \"340M\",\n447\t            \"20B\",\n448\t            \"58.8\",\n449\t            \"Under-trained (~0.3x Chinchilla)\"\n450\t          ],\n451\t          [\n452\t            \"1.3B\",\n453\t            \"1.3B\",\n454\t            \"100B\",\n455\t            \"76.9\",\n456\t            \"Under-trained (~0.4x Chinchilla)\"\n457\t          ]\n458\t        ]\n459\t      }\n460\t    ],\n461\t    \"figures\": [],\n462\t    \"analysis\": \"Claim 2 (scale-dependent convergence) is effectively dead as a standalone claim. The binary contrast is real — 340M doesn't converge, 1.3B does — but the attribution is hopelessly confounded. With the worst tier reframe, this becomes a reported observation rather than a claim: \\\"convergence was observed at 1.3B but not at 340M; however, these scales differ in both parameter count and training compute, preventing causal attribution.\\\" In the reframed paper focused on per-example divergence, scale becomes a secondary observation in a limitations paragraph rather than a headline claim. The GDN-specificity of the rebound further erodes confidence that it reflects a general scale phenomenon.\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, the 340M rebound is unexplained and unexplainable with available data. The fundamental confound is that 340M models were trained on 20B tokens while 1.3B models saw 100B tokens — a 5x difference. Tokens-per-parameter ratio also differs (58.8 vs 76.9), meaning 340M models are relatively undertrained. The rebound at high attention ratios could be: (a) insufficient capacity at 340M to benefit from many attention layers, (b) undertrained attention layers at 340M due to fewer tokens, (c) a genuine scale threshold effect. These hypotheses are indistinguishable without training 340M models on 100B tokens or 1.3B models on 20B tokens, neither of which is feasible. Per-example comparison shows low overlap between 340M and 1.3B rebound samples (Jaccard 0.12), and the rebound is most pronounced for GDN, again raising the GDN-specific confound. RetNet and HGRN2 at 340M show essentially flat trajectories with no clear rebound, making the \\\"rebound\\\" primarily a GDN phenomenon. The scale story collapses into: \\\"we observe a difference between 340M and 1.3B but cannot attribute it to scale, training compute, or their interaction.\\\"\\n\"\n463\t  },\n464\t  {\n465\t    \"name\": \"Statistical robustness framework\",\n466\t    \"description\": \"Bootstrap CIs for convergence metrics. Multi-comparison correction. Sensitivity analysis with GDN excluded. Effect sizes.\\n\",\n467\t    \"results\": {\n468\t      \"bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_full\": {\n469\t        \"point\": 0.79,\n470\t        \"ci_lower\": 0.61,\n471\t        \"ci_upper\": 0.91\n472\t      },\n473\t      \"bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_gdn_excluded\": {\n474\t        \"point\": 0.18,\n475\t        \"ci_lower\": -0.08,\n476\t        \"ci_upper\": 0.41\n477\t      },\n478\t      \"gdn_excluded_rho\": -0.6,\n479\t      \"gdn_excluded_p\": 0.285,\n480\t      \"gdn_excluded_significant\": false,\n481\t      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_trajectory\": \"non-monotonic\",\n482\t      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h0\": 0.021,\n483\t      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h3\": 0.019,\n484\t      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h6\": 0.018,\n485\t      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h12\": 0.02,\n486\t      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h24\": 0.017,\n487\t      \"bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada\": 0.048,\n488\t      \"bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada_6tests\": 0.288,\n489\t      \"fdr_corrected_significant_benchmarks\": 0,\n490\t      \"cohens_d_std_reduction_full\": 1.82,\n491\t      \"cohens_d_std_reduction_gdn_excluded\": 0.34\n492\t    },\n493\t    \"tables\": [\n494\t      {\n495\t        \"title\": \"Sensitivity analysis: GDN inclusion/exclusion on LAMBADA convergence\",\n496\t        \"columns\": [\n497\t          \"Metric\",\n498\t          \"All 3 architectures\",\n499\t          \"GDN excluded (RetNet + HGRN2 only)\"\n500\t        ],\n501\t        \"rows\": [\n502\t          [\n503\t            \"Std reduction (%)\",\n504\t            \"79%\",\n505\t            \"18%\"\n506\t          ],\n507\t          [\n508\t            \"Bootstrap 95% CI\",\n509\t            \"[0.61, 0.91]\",\n510\t            \"[-0.08, 0.41]\"\n511\t          ],\n512\t          [\n513\t            \"Spearman ρ (std vs attn ratio)\",\n514\t            \"-1.00\",\n515\t            \"-0.60\"\n516\t          ],\n517\t          [\n518\t            \"p-value (two-tailed)\",\n519\t            \"0.008\",\n520\t            \"0.285\"\n521\t          ],\n522\t          [\n523\t            \"Cohen's d\",\n524\t            \"1.82\",\n525\t            \"0.34\"\n526\t          ],\n527\t          [\n528\t            \"Monotonic decrease?\",\n529\t            \"Yes\",\n530\t            \"No (rebound at h=12)\"\n531\t          ]\n532\t        ]\n533\t      },\n534\t      {\n535\t        \"title\": \"Multiple comparison correction across benchmarks\",\n536\t        \"columns\": [\n537\t          \"Benchmark\",\n538\t          \"Raw p\",\n539\t          \"Bonferroni p (×6)\",\n540\t          \"FDR-adjusted p\",\n541\t          \"Significant at α=0.05\"\n542\t        ],\n543\t        \"rows\": [\n544\t          [\n545\t            \"LAMBADA\",\n546\t            \"0.008\",\n547\t            \"0.048\",\n548\t            \"0.048\",\n549\t            \"Borderline / No\"\n550\t          ],\n551\t          [\n552\t            \"ARC-Easy\",\n553\t            \"0.117\",\n554\t            \"0.702\",\n555\t            \"0.351\",\n556\t            \"No\"\n557\t          ],\n558\t          [\n559\t            \"MMLU\",\n560\t            \"0.117\",\n561\t            \"0.702\",\n562\t            \"0.351\",\n563\t            \"No\"\n564\t          ],\n565\t          [\n566\t            \"HellaSwag\",\n567\t            \"0.742\",\n568\t            \"1.000\",\n569\t            \"0.891\",\n570\t            \"No\"\n571\t          ],\n572\t          [\n573\t            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n574\t            \"0.883\",\n575\t            \"1.000\",\n576\t            \"0.883\",\n577\t            \"No\"\n578\t          ],\n579\t          [\n580\t            \"WinoGrande\",\n581\t            \"0.608\",\n582\t            \"1.000\",\n583\t            \"0.891\",\n584\t            \"No\"\n585\t          ]\n586\t        ]\n587\t      },\n588\t      {\n589\t        \"title\": \"RetNet-HGRN2 cross-architecture std trajectory (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\",\n590\t        \"columns\": [\n591\t          \"Attention layers\",\n592\t          \"RetNet acc\",\n593\t          \"HGRN2 acc\",\n594\t          \"Cross-arch std\",\n595\t          \"Δ from h=0\"\n596\t        ],\n597\t        \"rows\": [\n598\t          [\n599\t            \"h=0\",\n600\t            \"0.412\",\n601\t            \"0.391\",\n602\t            \"0.0148\",\n603\t            \"—\"\n604\t          ],\n605\t          [\n606\t            \"h=3\",\n607\t            \"0.428\",\n608\t            \"0.414\",\n609\t            \"0.0099\",\n610\t            \"-33%\"\n611\t          ],\n612\t          [\n613\t            \"h=6\",\n614\t            \"0.441\",\n615\t            \"0.422\",\n616\t            \"0.0134\",\n617\t            \"-9%\"\n618\t          ],\n619\t          [\n620\t            \"h=12\",\n621\t            \"0.455\",\n622\t            \"0.437\",\n623\t            \"0.0127\",\n624\t            \"-14%\"\n625\t          ],\n626\t          [\n627\t            \"h=24\",\n628\t            \"0.468\",\n629\t            \"0.456\",\n630\t            \"0.0085\",\n631\t            \"-43%\"\n632\t          ]\n633\t        ]\n634\t      }\n635\t    ],\n636\t    \"figures\": [],\n637\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the experiment that forces the reframe. The convergence finding does not survive the most basic robustness check — removing one of three architectures. A reviewer who asks \\\"is this just GDN being weird?\\\" can point to this table and say yes. The Bonferroni correction is also damaging: even LAMBADA barely survives at 0.048, and a strict reviewer would reject it. The paper must be transparent about this. In the reframed narrative, this analysis appears in Section 4 as: \\\"We initially observed strong aggregate convergence, but robustness analysis reveals this is substantially driven by a single architecture (GDN). We therefore focus on the more robust finding: per-example divergence, which persists regardless of GDN's inclusion.\\\" This honesty actually strengthens the paper — it shows rigorous self-evaluation.\\n\\n\\nThis is the core worst-tier finding. When GDN is excluded, the 79% std reduction collapses to 18% — and the bootstrap 95% CI crosses zero ([-0.08, 0.41]). The RetNet-HGRN2 pair alone shows a flat-to-slightly-decreasing std trajectory that is non-monotonic (slight increase at h=12 before decreasing again at h=24). The Spearman ρ drops from -1.0 to -0.60 (p=0.285), losing significance entirely. This means the convergence finding is substantially driven by GDN's anomalous behavior — either the D004 confound or a GDN-specific architectural artifact. Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks kills even LAMBADA's significance (0.048 × 6 = 0.288). FDR correction yields zero significant benchmarks. The convergence claim, as originally stated, does not survive robustness checks. Cohen's d drops from 1.82 (large, with GDN) to 0.34 (small, without GDN), confirming the effect is GDN-driven. The paper CANNOT lead with convergence. The reframe to per-example divergence is not optional — it's necessary.\\n\"\n638\t  },\n639\t  {\n640\t    \"name\": \"Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\",\n641\t    \"description\": \"Plot aggregate convergence alongside per-example divergence metrics (Jaccard, net effect asymmetry) as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Test whether convergence and divergence co-evolve.\\n\",\n642\t    \"results\": {\n643\t      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_rho\": -0.3,\n644\t      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_p\": 0.45,\n645\t      \"jaccard_trend_with_attention\": \"stable\",\n646\t      \"jaccard_at_h0\": null,\n647\t      \"jaccard_at_h3\": 0.08,\n648\t      \"jaccard_at_h6\": 0.07,\n649\t      \"jaccard_at_h12\": 0.09,\n650\t      \"jaccard_at_h24\": 0.07,\n651\t      \"net_effect_asymmetry_stable\": true,\n652\t      \"paradox_visualization_compelling\": \"moderate\",\n653\t      \"co_evolution_significant\": false\n654\t    },\n655\t    \"tables\": [\n656\t      {\n657\t        \"title\": \"Convergence-divergence metrics by attention ratio (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\",\n658\t        \"columns\": [\n659\t          \"Attention ratio (h/24)\",\n660\t          \"Cross-arch std (all 3)\",\n661\t          \"Cross-arch std (no GDN)\",\n662\t          \"Mean pairwise Jaccard\",\n663\t          \"Net effect asymmetry\"\n664\t        ],\n665\t        \"rows\": [\n666\t          [\n667\t            \"0/24 (h=0)\",\n668\t            \"0.024\",\n669\t            \"0.015\",\n670\t            \"—\",\n671\t            \"—\"\n672\t          ],\n673\t          [\n674\t            \"3/24 (h=3)\",\n675\t            \"0.018\",\n676\t            \"0.010\",\n677\t            \"0.08\",\n678\t            \"0.42\"\n679\t          ],\n680\t          [\n681\t            \"6/24 (h=6)\",\n682\t            \"0.012\",\n683\t            \"0.013\",\n684\t            \"0.07\",\n685\t            \"0.45\"\n686\t          ],\n687\t          [\n688\t            \"12/24 (h=12)\",\n689\t            \"0.008\",\n690\t            \"0.013\",\n691\t            \"0.09\",\n692\t            \"0.39\"\n693\t          ],\n694\t          [\n695\t            \"24/24 (h=24)\",\n696\t            \"0.005\",\n697\t            \"0.009\",\n698\t            \"0.07\",\n699\t            \"0.44\"\n700\t          ]\n701\t        ]\n702\t      },\n703\t      {\n704\t        \"title\": \"Convergence-divergence correlation\",\n705\t        \"columns\": [\n706\t          \"Metric pair\",\n707\t          \"Spearman ρ\",\n708\t          \"p-value\",\n709\t          \"Interpretation\"\n710\t        ],\n711\t        \"rows\": [\n712\t          [\n713\t            \"Std (all 3) vs Jaccard\",\n714\t            \"-0.30\",\n715\t            \"0.45\",\n716\t            \"Not significant\"\n717\t          ],\n718\t          [\n719\t            \"Std (no GDN) vs Jaccard\",\n720\t            \"-0.10\",\n721\t            \"0.82\",\n722\t            \"No relationship\"\n723\t          ],\n724\t          [\n725\t            \"Std (all 3) vs net asymmetry\",\n726\t            \"0.10\",\n727\t            \"0.87\",\n728\t            \"No relationship\"\n729\t          ]\n730\t        ]\n731\t      }\n732\t    ],\n733\t    \"figures\": [],\n734\t    \"analysis\": \"The paradox framing survives only as a visual observation when GDN is included, not as a statistically supported co-evolution. In the reframed paper, this becomes the key Figure 1: \\\"Per-example divergence is architecture-intrinsic and attention-invariant.\\\" The flat Jaccard line is actually the strongest visual argument for the reframed paper — it shows that no amount of attention makes architectures agree on which examples to solve. This is more powerful than a co-evolution story because it's simpler and more robust. The \\\"paradox\\\" terminology should be softened to \\\"disconnect\\\" or \\\"dissociation\\\" — aggregate metrics move while per-example identity doesn't. The Sankey diagram showing stable sample fates across attention ratios could be a compelling supplementary figure.\\n\\nIn the worst tier, the convergence-divergence \\\"co-evolution\\\" is not significant because the convergence side is broken (GDN-driven). Jaccard remains stable across attention ratios (~0.07-0.09), meaning per-example divergence is always present regardless of how much attention is added. This is actually consistent with the reframed narrative but kills the \\\"paradox\\\" framing: there's no co-evolution because divergence is a constant, not a function of attention ratio. The correlation between aggregate convergence (which is noisy without GDN) and per-example Jaccard is weak (ρ=-0.30, p=0.45). The \\\"paradox\\\" becomes less of a paradox and more of a simple observation: architectures always solve different examples (Jaccard ~0.08 is intrinsic), and sometimes their aggregate scores happen to converge (when GDN is included). The visualization can still be striking — showing flat Jaccard against a converging (or not) std line — but the causal \\\"more attention = more paradox\\\" story doesn't hold.\\n\"\n735\t  }\n736\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ad887cb-6bfe-4b1f-aec5-d829b0b82a82","uuid":"989578e1-460b-4d3d-bd5b-e7f025de551c","timestamp":"2026-05-19T14:12:51.323Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_2/projects/entrainment_gated_ft/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260519T135121/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multi-benchmark per-example analysis (task-specificity validation)\",\n    \"description\": \"Re-run lm-eval with --log_samples on 1.3B models for HellaSwag, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge. Collect per-example correctness vectors across architectures and attention ratios. Compute pairwise Jaccard on helped/hurt sets; test whether per-example convergence is absent on non-LAMBADA benchmarks.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"hellaswag_jaccard_mean\": 0.08,\n      \"hellaswag_jaccard_std\": 0.03,\n      \"arc_easy_jaccard_mean\": 0.11,\n      \"arc_challenge_jaccard_mean\": 0.13,\n      \"hellaswag_per_example_convergence_rho\": -0.15,\n      \"hellaswag_per_example_convergence_p\": 0.62,\n      \"arc_easy_per_example_convergence_rho\": -0.4,\n      \"arc_easy_per_example_convergence_p\": 0.35,\n      \"winogrande_per_example_convergence_rho\": -0.2,\n      \"winogrande_per_example_convergence_p\": 0.55,\n      \"n_benchmarks_with_per_example_convergence\": 0,\n      \"arc_easy_aggregate_convergence_suggestive\": true,\n      \"arc_easy_per_example_mechanism_matches_lambada\": false\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Per-example convergence metrics across benchmarks (1.3B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"Aggregate ρ (std vs attn)\",\n          \"Per-example ρ\",\n          \"Per-example p\",\n          \"Mean Jaccard\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"LAMBADA\",\n            \"-1.00\",\n            \"-0.90\",\n            \"0.017\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"Convergent (but GDN-driven)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Easy\",\n            \"-0.70\",\n            \"-0.40\",\n            \"0.350\",\n            \"0.11\",\n            \"Weak signal, non-significant\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n            \"-0.30\",\n            \"-0.10\",\n            \"0.780\",\n            \"0.13\",\n            \"No convergence\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"-0.15\",\n            \"0.620\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"No convergence\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"WinoGrande\",\n            \"0.10\",\n            \"-0.20\",\n            \"0.550\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"No convergence\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Pairwise Jaccard similarity on helped sets (1.3B, h3-1 config)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"GDN-RetNet\",\n          \"GDN-HGRN2\",\n          \"RetNet-HGRN2\",\n          \"Mean\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"LAMBADA\",\n            \"0.06\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"0.07\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Easy\",\n            \"0.10\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.11\",\n            \"0.11\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n            \"0.11\",\n            \"0.14\",\n            \"0.13\",\n            \"0.13\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"0.08\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"WinoGrande\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"0.10\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"0.09\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ARC-Easy per-example signal (ρ=-0.40) is the worst-case complication. It's not significant (p=0.35) but it's not zero either. A reviewer can argue: \\\"convergence exists weakly everywhere, LAMBADA just happens to cross significance because GDN's anomalous behavior inflates the signal.\\\" This undermines the retrieval-specificity framing. However, the universal low Jaccard (all <0.15) is the paper's salvation — it doesn't depend on convergence at all. The reframe: \\\"regardless of whether architectures converge in aggregate, they solve different examples\\\" is supported by every benchmark. Dose-response narrative for convergence strength is dead; replace with: \\\"per-example divergence is the invariant, convergence is the variable.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, per-example analysis on HellaSwag and WinoGrande reveals NO convergence signal at sample level either — confirming task-specificity holds. However, ARC-Easy shows a weak but non-trivial per-example signal (rho=-0.40, p=0.35) that partially mirrors LAMBADA's pattern, threatening the clean \\\"retrieval-specific\\\" story. The effect is too weak to be significant but too present to ignore, collapsing the task-specificity claim into a muddier \\\"convergence is generic but weak everywhere except LAMBADA where GDN amplifies it\\\" narrative. Jaccard values on HellaSwag (~0.08) confirm per-example divergence generalizes across benchmarks — this is the one robust finding. ARC tasks show slightly higher Jaccard (0.11-0.13), suggesting reasoning tasks have marginally more overlap in architecture behavior but still fundamentally low. The critical implication: convergence is NOT cleanly retrieval-specific (undermining Claim 1's dose-response framing), but per-example divergence IS universal (supporting the reframed narrative).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Helped/hurt sample characterization — Phase 2 (perplexity + topic)\",\n    \"description\": \"Extend Phase 1 surface feature analysis with deeper features: (a) perplexity of target token under pure recurrent baseline (no attention), (b) topic/domain classification of LAMBADA contexts using a lightweight classifier or embedding clustering. Test whether these features differentiate helped vs hurt samples within and across architectures.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"perplexity_gdn_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.18,\n      \"perplexity_hgrn2_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.09,\n      \"perplexity_retnet_helped_vs_hurt_p\": 0.31,\n      \"perplexity_cross_arch_interaction_p\": 0.42,\n      \"topic_gdn_chi2_p\": 0.35,\n      \"topic_hgrn2_chi2_p\": 0.22,\n      \"topic_retnet_chi2_p\": 0.47,\n      \"topic_cross_arch_difference_p\": 0.61,\n      \"any_feature_significant_p_lt_01\": false,\n      \"n_features_marginal_p_lt_05\": 1,\n      \"best_single_feature_auc\": 0.54\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Phase 2 feature analysis: helped vs hurt samples (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Feature\",\n          \"GDN p-value\",\n          \"HGRN2 p-value\",\n          \"RetNet p-value\",\n          \"Cross-arch interaction p\",\n          \"Best AUC\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Baseline perplexity\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"0.31\",\n            \"0.42\",\n            \"0.54\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Topic (fiction/non-fiction)\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"0.22\",\n            \"0.47\",\n            \"0.61\",\n            \"0.51\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Topic (10-cluster)\",\n            \"0.41\",\n            \"0.29\",\n            \"0.53\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"0.52\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Token frequency (Phase 1)\",\n            \">0.2\",\n            \">0.2\",\n            \">0.2\",\n            \">0.5\",\n            \"0.51\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Context length (Phase 1)\",\n            \">0.2\",\n            \">0.2\",\n            \"0.012\",\n            \">0.5\",\n            \"0.52\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Combined feature model (logistic regression, helped vs hurt)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Architecture\",\n          \"All features AUC\",\n          \"95% CI\",\n          \"Permutation baseline AUC\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN\",\n            \"0.53\",\n            \"[0.49, 0.57]\",\n            \"0.50\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HGRN2\",\n            \"0.55\",\n            \"[0.51, 0.59]\",\n            \"0.50\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RetNet\",\n            \"0.52\",\n            \"[0.48, 0.56]\",\n            \"0.50\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Complete mechanistic failure. Six features tested across two phases, none reaches significance with cross-architecture consistency. The HGRN2 perplexity trend (p=0.09) is tantalizing but unreliable — it doesn't survive multiple comparison correction and doesn't generalize to the other architectures. The combined model AUC of 0.53-0.55 means that even throwing all features together, we predict helped/hurt status barely better than a coin flip. This is simultaneously the worst and most interesting outcome: the architecture-specific effect is NOT driven by any obvious data property. For the reframed paper, this becomes: \\\"standard sample features cannot explain which examples each architecture handles differently, suggesting the divergence operates at a representational level not captured by surface or moderate-depth features.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, ALL Phase 2 features are non-significant. Perplexity under baseline shows a marginal trend for HGRN2 (p=0.09) — helped samples tend to have slightly higher baseline perplexity, suggesting attention helps on \\\"harder\\\" items — but the effect doesn't replicate across GDN (p=0.18) or RetNet (p=0.31), and the cross-architecture interaction is nowhere near significant (p=0.42). Topic/domain analysis reveals no systematic pattern at all (all p>0.2). The best single-feature AUC for predicting helped/hurt is 0.54, barely above chance. This means we have ZERO mechanistic toehold: we cannot explain what makes a sample helped vs hurt by any measurable property. The architecture-specific effect is real (Jaccard proves it) but opaque. This is the worst case for Concern C — no causal mechanism is articulable. The reframe must embrace this opacity: \\\"the effect is detectable but not yet explainable, which itself is a finding about the limits of current interpretability.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Attention weight extraction and visualization\",\n    \"description\": \"Extract attention weight matrices from h3-1 hybrid models for all 3 architectures on LAMBADA samples. Analyze: position-level attention entropy, correlation between attention focus and helped/hurt identity, cross-architecture attention pattern similarity (CKA or cosine).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_gdn\": 0.07,\n      \"attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_hgrn2\": 0.12,\n      \"attention_entropy_helped_vs_hurt_correlation_retnet\": 0.04,\n      \"cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_retnet\": 0.31,\n      \"cross_arch_attention_cka_gdn_hgrn2\": 0.28,\n      \"cross_arch_attention_cka_retnet_hgrn2\": 0.35,\n      \"attention_focus_position_correlation_with_effect\": 0.09,\n      \"attention_pattern_predicts_helped_auc\": 0.56,\n      \"interpretable_pattern_found\": false\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Attention pattern analysis (LAMBADA, 1.3B h3-1)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"GDN\",\n          \"HGRN2\",\n          \"RetNet\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Mean attention entropy (helped)\",\n            \"2.31\",\n            \"2.45\",\n            \"2.28\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mean attention entropy (hurt)\",\n            \"2.34\",\n            \"2.39\",\n            \"2.30\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Entropy correlation with effect size\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.04\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"% attention on last 10 tokens (helped)\",\n            \"0.34\",\n            \"0.29\",\n            \"0.36\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"% attention on last 10 tokens (hurt)\",\n            \"0.33\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.35\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Attention predicts helped AUC\",\n            \"0.54\",\n            \"0.57\",\n            \"0.53\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Cross-architecture attention similarity (CKA, h3-1 layer)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Pair\",\n          \"CKA (all samples)\",\n          \"CKA (helped subset)\",\n          \"CKA (hurt subset)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN–RetNet\",\n            \"0.31\",\n            \"0.29\",\n            \"0.33\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GDN–HGRN2\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.26\",\n            \"0.30\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RetNet–HGRN2\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"0.33\",\n            \"0.37\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Attention analysis provides no mechanistic explanation. The moderate CKA values (~0.30) are actually a nuisance finding — attention is somewhat similar across architectures, which means the divergent per-example outcomes must arise from the INTERACTION between attention and the different recurrence mechanisms, not from attention alone. This is plausible but untestable within our experimental scope — we'd need to ablate the recurrence mechanism while holding attention fixed, which requires retraining. For the reframed paper, this section becomes: \\\"attention patterns alone do not explain architecture-specific effects, suggesting the mechanism operates through attention-recurrence interaction\\\" — a honest null result that points to future work. The figures can still appear in the paper as evidence of thoroughness.\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, attention patterns show low correlation with helped/hurt identity (r<0.15 for all architectures). Attention entropy — a measure of how focused vs diffuse the attention is — differs only trivially between helped and hurt samples. Cross-architecture CKA similarity is moderate (~0.30), meaning attention layers are doing somewhat similar things structurally across architectures, but this structural similarity does NOT predict the divergent per-example outcomes. The attention pattern AUC for predicting helped/hurt (0.56) is only marginally better than the feature-based model and far from interpretable. No clear \\\"smoking gun\\\" pattern emerges (e.g., no finding like \\\"GDN attends to nearby context while RetNet attends to distant context on helped samples\\\"). This makes Concern C (no causal mechanism) essentially unanswerable within the scope of this paper. The attention layer is doing something architecture-specific, but what it's doing doesn't cleanly map onto which examples benefit.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Architecture expansion check\",\n    \"description\": \"Search Wang et al. model zoo and public repositories for additional hybrid architectures (Mamba-2 hybrids, RWKV hybrids, Griffin, etc.) compatible with lm-eval. If found, evaluate on LAMBADA to test convergence generalization.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"additional_architectures_found\": 0,\n      \"additional_architectures_compatible\": 0,\n      \"n_architectures_tested\": 3,\n      \"wang_et_al_only_gdn_retnet_hgrn2\": true,\n      \"public_hybrid_checkpoints_available\": false\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Architecture expansion search results\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Architecture\",\n          \"Source\",\n          \"Hybrid checkpoints available\",\n          \"Compatible\",\n          \"Reason if incompatible\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Mamba-2 hybrid\",\n            \"Waleffe et al. 2024\",\n            \"Yes (Zamba)\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"Different tokenizer, training data, no attention-ratio sweep\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Griffin\",\n            \"De et al. 2024\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"Checkpoints not public\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RWKV-6 hybrid\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"No hybrid variant exists\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Based (linear attention)\",\n            \"Arora et al. 2024\",\n            \"Partial\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"No systematic attention-ratio sweep\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Hawk/Griffin (RecurrentGemma)\",\n            \"Google 2024\",\n            \"Yes (2B)\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"Single scale, single config, no attention sweep\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Architecture expansion is impossible within constraints. The paper is locked at n=3. The reframed narrative partially mitigates this: if the headline claim is \\\"per-example divergence exists\\\" rather than \\\"convergence is universal,\\\" then n=3 is sufficient to demonstrate the phenomenon. The generalization question (\\\"does this hold for all hybrid architectures?\\\") becomes explicit future work. Honest framing: \\\"We demonstrate the phenomenon across three architecturally distinct recurrence mechanisms; whether it extends to all hybrid architectures is an open question requiring controlled training of additional architecture families.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, no additional architectures are available. Wang et al.'s model zoo contains only GDN, RetNet, and HGRN2 — confirmed by their paper and repository. Public Mamba-2 hybrid checkpoints exist but were trained with incompatible tokenizers or training setups, making fair comparison impossible without retraining (out of GPU budget). Griffin checkpoints are not publicly released. RWKV-6 hybrids don't exist in the literature. The n=3 limitation is hard and cannot be resolved before the ARR deadline. This is a genuine limitation that reviewers will flag, and the paper must address it directly. Mitigation: emphasize that the three architectures represent three DISTINCT recurrence mechanisms (gated delta rule, linear attention + exponential decay, hierarchical gated recurrence), so the finding spans mechanism diversity even if not architecture count.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"340M rebound mechanism analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Deep analysis of 340M non-monotonic rebound at attn=8. Per-example comparison with 1.3B at same attention ratio. Attempt to disentangle scale (340M vs 1.3B) from training tokens (20B vs 100B) as explanatory variables for the rebound.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"rebound_explained\": false,\n      \"scale_vs_tokens_disentangleable\": false,\n      \"per_example_overlap_340m_1_3b_jaccard\": 0.12,\n      \"rebound_samples_characterizable\": false,\n      \"rebound_unique_to_gdn\": true,\n      \"retnet_hgrn2_340m_trajectory\": \"flat\",\n      \"training_tokens_340m\": \"20B\",\n      \"training_tokens_1_3b\": \"100B\",\n      \"tokens_per_param_340m\": 58.8,\n      \"tokens_per_param_1_3b\": 76.9\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"340M vs 1.3B trajectory comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Architecture\",\n          \"340M trajectory\",\n          \"1.3B trajectory\",\n          \"340M rebound at h=8\",\n          \"Confound\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"GDN\",\n            \"Non-monotonic (rebound at h=8)\",\n            \"Monotonic convergence\",\n            \"Yes (std increases 40%)\",\n            \"D004 confound + undertrained\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"RetNet\",\n            \"Flat (no clear trend)\",\n            \"Monotonic convergence\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HGRN2\",\n            \"Flat (slight improvement)\",\n            \"Monotonic convergence\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"20B tokens may be insufficient\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Training configuration confound\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Scale\",\n          \"Params\",\n          \"Training tokens\",\n          \"Tokens/param\",\n          \"Chinchilla ratio\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"340M\",\n            \"340M\",\n            \"20B\",\n            \"58.8\",\n            \"Under-trained (~0.3x Chinchilla)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1.3B\",\n            \"1.3B\",\n            \"100B\",\n            \"76.9\",\n            \"Under-trained (~0.4x Chinchilla)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Claim 2 (scale-dependent convergence) is effectively dead as a standalone claim. The binary contrast is real — 340M doesn't converge, 1.3B does — but the attribution is hopelessly confounded. With the worst tier reframe, this becomes a reported observation rather than a claim: \\\"convergence was observed at 1.3B but not at 340M; however, these scales differ in both parameter count and training compute, preventing causal attribution.\\\" In the reframed paper focused on per-example divergence, scale becomes a secondary observation in a limitations paragraph rather than a headline claim. The GDN-specificity of the rebound further erodes confidence that it reflects a general scale phenomenon.\\n\\n\\nIn the worst tier, the 340M rebound is unexplained and unexplainable with available data. The fundamental confound is that 340M models were trained on 20B tokens while 1.3B models saw 100B tokens — a 5x difference. Tokens-per-parameter ratio also differs (58.8 vs 76.9), meaning 340M models are relatively undertrained. The rebound at high attention ratios could be: (a) insufficient capacity at 340M to benefit from many attention layers, (b) undertrained attention layers at 340M due to fewer tokens, (c) a genuine scale threshold effect. These hypotheses are indistinguishable without training 340M models on 100B tokens or 1.3B models on 20B tokens, neither of which is feasible. Per-example comparison shows low overlap between 340M and 1.3B rebound samples (Jaccard 0.12), and the rebound is most pronounced for GDN, again raising the GDN-specific confound. RetNet and HGRN2 at 340M show essentially flat trajectories with no clear rebound, making the \\\"rebound\\\" primarily a GDN phenomenon. The scale story collapses into: \\\"we observe a difference between 340M and 1.3B but cannot attribute it to scale, training compute, or their interaction.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Statistical robustness framework\",\n    \"description\": \"Bootstrap CIs for convergence metrics. Multi-comparison correction. Sensitivity analysis with GDN excluded. Effect sizes.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_full\": {\n        \"point\": 0.79,\n        \"ci_lower\": 0.61,\n        \"ci_upper\": 0.91\n      },\n      \"bootstrap_ci_std_reduction_gdn_excluded\": {\n        \"point\": 0.18,\n        \"ci_lower\": -0.08,\n        \"ci_upper\": 0.41\n      },\n      \"gdn_excluded_rho\": -0.6,\n      \"gdn_excluded_p\": 0.285,\n      \"gdn_excluded_significant\": false,\n      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_trajectory\": \"non-monotonic\",\n      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h0\": 0.021,\n      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h3\": 0.019,\n      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h6\": 0.018,\n      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h12\": 0.02,\n      \"retnet_hgrn2_std_at_h24\": 0.017,\n      \"bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada\": 0.048,\n      \"bonferroni_corrected_p_lambada_6tests\": 0.288,\n      \"fdr_corrected_significant_benchmarks\": 0,\n      \"cohens_d_std_reduction_full\": 1.82,\n      \"cohens_d_std_reduction_gdn_excluded\": 0.34\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Sensitivity analysis: GDN inclusion/exclusion on LAMBADA convergence\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"All 3 architectures\",\n          \"GDN excluded (RetNet + HGRN2 only)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Std reduction (%)\",\n            \"79%\",\n            \"18%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Bootstrap 95% CI\",\n            \"[0.61, 0.91]\",\n            \"[-0.08, 0.41]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spearman ρ (std vs attn ratio)\",\n            \"-1.00\",\n            \"-0.60\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"p-value (two-tailed)\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.285\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cohen's d\",\n            \"1.82\",\n            \"0.34\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Monotonic decrease?\",\n            \"Yes\",\n            \"No (rebound at h=12)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Multiple comparison correction across benchmarks\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Benchmark\",\n          \"Raw p\",\n          \"Bonferroni p (×6)\",\n          \"FDR-adjusted p\",\n          \"Significant at α=0.05\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"LAMBADA\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.048\",\n            \"0.048\",\n            \"Borderline / No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Easy\",\n            \"0.117\",\n            \"0.702\",\n            \"0.351\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            \"0.117\",\n            \"0.702\",\n            \"0.351\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HellaSwag\",\n            \"0.742\",\n            \"1.000\",\n            \"0.891\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n            \"0.883\",\n            \"1.000\",\n            \"0.883\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"WinoGrande\",\n            \"0.608\",\n            \"1.000\",\n            \"0.891\",\n            \"No\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"RetNet-HGRN2 cross-architecture std trajectory (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Attention layers\",\n          \"RetNet acc\",\n          \"HGRN2 acc\",\n          \"Cross-arch std\",\n          \"Δ from h=0\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"h=0\",\n            \"0.412\",\n            \"0.391\",\n            \"0.0148\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h=3\",\n            \"0.428\",\n            \"0.414\",\n            \"0.0099\",\n            \"-33%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h=6\",\n            \"0.441\",\n            \"0.422\",\n            \"0.0134\",\n            \"-9%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h=12\",\n            \"0.455\",\n            \"0.437\",\n            \"0.0127\",\n            \"-14%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"h=24\",\n            \"0.468\",\n            \"0.456\",\n            \"0.0085\",\n            \"-43%\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the experiment that forces the reframe. The convergence finding does not survive the most basic robustness check — removing one of three architectures. A reviewer who asks \\\"is this just GDN being weird?\\\" can point to this table and say yes. The Bonferroni correction is also damaging: even LAMBADA barely survives at 0.048, and a strict reviewer would reject it. The paper must be transparent about this. In the reframed narrative, this analysis appears in Section 4 as: \\\"We initially observed strong aggregate convergence, but robustness analysis reveals this is substantially driven by a single architecture (GDN). We therefore focus on the more robust finding: per-example divergence, which persists regardless of GDN's inclusion.\\\" This honesty actually strengthens the paper — it shows rigorous self-evaluation.\\n\\n\\nThis is the core worst-tier finding. When GDN is excluded, the 79% std reduction collapses to 18% — and the bootstrap 95% CI crosses zero ([-0.08, 0.41]). The RetNet-HGRN2 pair alone shows a flat-to-slightly-decreasing std trajectory that is non-monotonic (slight increase at h=12 before decreasing again at h=24). The Spearman ρ drops from -1.0 to -0.60 (p=0.285), losing significance entirely. This means the convergence finding is substantially driven by GDN's anomalous behavior — either the D004 confound or a GDN-specific architectural artifact. Bonferroni correction across 6 benchmarks kills even LAMBADA's significance (0.048 × 6 = 0.288). FDR correction yields zero significant benchmarks. The convergence claim, as originally stated, does not survive robustness checks. Cohen's d drops from 1.82 (large, with GDN) to 0.34 (small, without GDN), confirming the effect is GDN-driven. The paper CANNOT lead with convergence. The reframe to per-example divergence is not optional — it's necessary.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Convergence-divergence bridge analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Plot aggregate convergence alongside per-example divergence metrics (Jaccard, net effect asymmetry) as function of attention ratio for LAMBADA. Test whether convergence and divergence co-evolve.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_rho\": -0.3,\n      \"convergence_divergence_correlation_p\": 0.45,\n      \"jaccard_trend_with_attention\": \"stable\",\n      \"jaccard_at_h0\": null,\n      \"jaccard_at_h3\": 0.08,\n      \"jaccard_at_h6\": 0.07,\n      \"jaccard_at_h12\": 0.09,\n      \"jaccard_at_h24\": 0.07,\n      \"net_effect_asymmetry_stable\": true,\n      \"paradox_visualization_compelling\": \"moderate\",\n      \"co_evolution_significant\": false\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Convergence-divergence metrics by attention ratio (LAMBADA, 1.3B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Attention ratio (h/24)\",\n          \"Cross-arch std (all 3)\",\n          \"Cross-arch std (no GDN)\",\n          \"Mean pairwise Jaccard\",\n          \"Net effect asymmetry\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0/24 (h=0)\",\n            \"0.024\",\n            \"0.015\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3/24 (h=3)\",\n            \"0.018\",\n            \"0.010\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"0.42\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"6/24 (h=6)\",\n            \"0.012\",\n            \"0.013\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"0.45\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"12/24 (h=12)\",\n            \"0.008\",\n            \"0.013\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"0.39\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"24/24 (h=24)\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.009\",\n            \"0.07\",\n            \"0.44\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Convergence-divergence correlation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric pair\",\n          \"Spearman ρ\",\n          \"p-value\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Std (all 3) vs Jaccard\",\n            \"-0.30\",\n            \"0.45\",\n            \"Not significant\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Std (no GDN) vs Jaccard\",\n            \"-0.10\",\n            \"0.82\",\n            \"No relationship\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Std (all 3) vs net asymmetry\",\n            \"0.10\",\n            \"0.87\",\n            \"No relationship\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The paradox framing survives only as a visual observation when GDN is included, not as a statistically supported co-evolution. In the reframed paper, this becomes the key Figure 1: \\\"Per-example divergence is architecture-intrinsic and attention-invariant.\\\" The flat Jaccard line is actually the strongest visual argument for the reframed paper — it shows that no amount of attention makes architectures agree on which examples to solve. This is more powerful than a co-evolution story because it's simpler and more robust. The \\\"paradox\\\" terminology should be softened to \\\"disconnect\\\" or \\\"dissociation\\\" — aggregate metrics move while per-example identity doesn't. The Sankey diagram showing stable sample fates across attention ratios could be a compelling supplementary figure.\\n\\nIn the worst tier, the convergence-divergence \\\"co-evolution\\\" is not significant because the convergence side is broken (GDN-driven). Jaccard remains stable across attention ratios (~0.07-0.09), meaning per-example divergence is always present regardless of how much attention is added. This is actually consistent with the reframed narrative but kills the \\\"paradox\\\" framing: there's no co-evolution because divergence is a constant, not a function of attention ratio. The correlation between aggregate convergence (which is noisy without GDN) and per-example Jaccard is weak (ρ=-0.30, p=0.45). The \\\"paradox\\\" becomes less of a paradox and more of a simple observation: architectures always solve different examples (Jaccard ~0.08 is intrinsic), and sometimes their aggregate scores happen to converge (when GDN is included). The visualization can still be striking — showing flat Jaccard against a converging (or not) std line — but the causal \\\"more attention = more paradox\\\" story doesn't hold.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":736,"startLine":1,"totalLines":736}}}
